1
00:00:17,243 --> 00:00:17,984
[SPEAKER_01]: I thought maybe...

2
00:00:18,484 --> 00:00:21,927
[SPEAKER_01]: I was thinking about... in regards to...

3
00:00:22,367 --> 00:00:45,586
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know in which note Jnana Veda talks about seeing a bird and hearing it, and then thinking along the lines of it is the same thing that I'm seeing and hearing, even without experiencing the thing through two different senses, even just one, like seeing a thing, like a tree, a bird,

4
00:00:46,226 --> 00:00:51,228
[SPEAKER_01]: It is, it exists, and I see, like, the image of this thing.

5
00:00:52,509 --> 00:00:55,831
[SPEAKER_01]: There is this thing existing behind this image, which I'm seeing.

6
00:00:56,291 --> 00:00:56,491
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

7
00:00:57,171 --> 00:01:03,895
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's basically the the idea of the external objective world, independent of my experience of it.

8
00:01:04,595 --> 00:01:14,720
[SPEAKER_01]: But you realize it's a contradiction in terms, because if you're seeing it, it's experienced, and if it weren't experienced, you couldn't even talk about it.

9
00:01:15,502 --> 00:01:18,324
[SPEAKER_03]: But, how do you know, then, that there isn't a real world behind it?

10
00:01:20,165 --> 00:01:20,586
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't.

11
00:01:21,286 --> 00:01:22,147
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can only assume.

12
00:01:23,588 --> 00:01:26,250
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't know that there is, neither can you know that there isn't.

13
00:01:27,611 --> 00:01:28,231
[SPEAKER_03]: So, there might be.

14
00:01:29,192 --> 00:01:30,693
[SPEAKER_03]: So, then, it might not be a contradiction.

15
00:01:33,335 --> 00:01:34,656
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but you could never find out.

16
00:01:35,777 --> 00:01:41,060
[SPEAKER_03]: Sure, but that's one thing to not be able to find out, another thing is to say it's a contradiction.

17
00:01:42,061 --> 00:01:44,263
[SPEAKER_03]: Because contradiction requires you to know that there isn't.

18
00:01:46,215 --> 00:01:51,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, what I mean is that it's a contradiction thinking that you can talk about it or think about it.

19
00:01:52,479 --> 00:01:55,462
[SPEAKER_03]: But we are talking about it.

20
00:01:55,482 --> 00:01:56,622
[SPEAKER_03]: See, we are clarifying something.

21
00:01:57,003 --> 00:01:57,223
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

22
00:01:57,683 --> 00:02:10,812
[SPEAKER_03]: That external world, that you cannot know as independent of your experience, that you can only know on the basis of your experience.

23
00:02:12,734 --> 00:02:15,636
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, there is a contradictory aspect to something.

24
00:02:16,860 --> 00:02:23,123
[SPEAKER_03]: What I'm saying is, you can't say there isn't that world out there, because you don't know that.

25
00:02:23,863 --> 00:02:25,944
[SPEAKER_03]: And you can't say that there is, because you don't know that.

26
00:02:26,585 --> 00:02:28,465
[SPEAKER_03]: All you know is the result of the experience.

27
00:02:29,006 --> 00:02:31,547
[SPEAKER_03]: Because, see, you can start saying, no, there is the public world.

28
00:02:31,887 --> 00:02:34,228
[SPEAKER_03]: Form and objects of the senses are real as such.

29
00:02:34,468 --> 00:02:37,129
[SPEAKER_03]: We all perceive them as collectively.

30
00:02:37,150 --> 00:02:40,451
[SPEAKER_03]: In my experience, it's just the sensory impingements of these forms.

31
00:02:41,552 --> 00:02:44,013
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, you could say, oh, there isn't such a world.

32
00:02:45,115 --> 00:02:46,096
[SPEAKER_03]: It's all in my mind.

33
00:02:46,536 --> 00:02:47,617
[SPEAKER_03]: It's all my experience.

34
00:02:47,717 --> 00:02:48,938
[SPEAKER_03]: Such world cannot exist.

35
00:02:50,018 --> 00:02:51,820
[SPEAKER_03]: But that would also be wrong now to say.

36
00:02:53,061 --> 00:02:56,943
[SPEAKER_03]: So, both go a bit too far.

37
00:02:59,525 --> 00:03:02,627
[SPEAKER_03]: So, where is the contradictory aspect in regard to those two statements?

38
00:03:02,847 --> 00:03:03,768
[SPEAKER_03]: What is the contradiction?

39
00:03:03,788 --> 00:03:07,511
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's not the existence or non-existence of the world, because that cannot be known.

40
00:03:08,291 --> 00:03:11,774
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you don't know whether there is something outside of yourself or there isn't.

41
00:03:12,054 --> 00:03:12,694
[SPEAKER_02]: The mistake is

42
00:03:14,048 --> 00:03:20,170
[SPEAKER_02]: Is this, in that statement, this self, this extra-temporal... Yeah.

43
00:03:20,230 --> 00:03:22,971
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the mistake, the emphasis is on your conceivings.

44
00:03:23,111 --> 00:03:27,193
[SPEAKER_03]: And the mistake there is, see, I don't know whether there is something outside or not.

45
00:03:27,553 --> 00:03:29,054
[SPEAKER_03]: Means I am assuming outside.

46
00:03:29,094 --> 00:03:29,474
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

47
00:03:30,314 --> 00:03:32,815
[SPEAKER_03]: I just cannot decide on the content of that outside.

48
00:03:33,615 --> 00:03:40,198
[SPEAKER_03]: Yet, that outside assumption is only known on the basis of this internal experience by me.

49
00:03:41,272 --> 00:03:43,873
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the notion of outside is a contradiction in terms.

50
00:03:44,474 --> 00:03:49,596
[SPEAKER_03]: Not the world, or not the world, or the world exists, or doesn't exist, or both exist, or neither exists.

51
00:03:50,156 --> 00:03:52,938
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's exactly why Buddha would not answer those questions in the suttas.

52
00:03:53,538 --> 00:03:54,799
[SPEAKER_03]: Or is the world eternal?

53
00:03:54,819 --> 00:03:54,959
[SPEAKER_03]: i.e.

54
00:03:55,039 --> 00:03:59,061
[SPEAKER_03]: does it exist on its own, independent of our experiences and the individual's?

55
00:03:59,561 --> 00:04:00,781
[SPEAKER_03]: Does it carry on its own?

56
00:04:01,522 --> 00:04:04,043
[SPEAKER_03]: Or is the world then not eternal?

57
00:04:04,263 --> 00:04:07,685
[SPEAKER_03]: And he wouldn't answer that, because either way you assume the outside.

58
00:04:08,405 --> 00:04:18,891
[SPEAKER_03]: You assume there is this space outside of your experience as a whole, inside of your sixth sense base, which is then populated by these forms and so on, or not.

59
00:04:20,572 --> 00:04:29,137
[SPEAKER_03]: So, just the conceiving of outside in the basis of your statement, there is the world outside, there isn't the world outside, your conceiving of the outside is the problem.

60
00:04:30,678 --> 00:04:31,778
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the contradiction.

61
00:04:33,539 --> 00:04:34,840
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so the issue...

62
00:04:36,028 --> 00:04:38,030
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that you think that you can refer to it.

63
00:04:38,310 --> 00:04:39,472
[SPEAKER_01]: Will you not say that?

64
00:04:39,752 --> 00:04:41,113
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, no, no, no.

65
00:04:41,153 --> 00:04:49,322
[SPEAKER_03]: You can refer to it, you can think it, but you don't necessarily have to conceive it as an outside, as first and this second to it.

66
00:04:51,198 --> 00:04:53,119
[SPEAKER_03]: You have to start seeing it as a phenomenon.

67
00:04:53,220 --> 00:04:57,122
[SPEAKER_03]: So, when you're referring to the outside, you recognize that that's within.

68
00:04:57,943 --> 00:05:09,151
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the point where I'm kind of trying to sort of emphasize here is, you should not start believing that I must stop thinking about outside, because every thinking of outside is the conceiving.

69
00:05:09,772 --> 00:05:11,513
[SPEAKER_03]: You can't stop thinking about outside.

70
00:05:11,653 --> 00:05:12,594
[SPEAKER_03]: You are the outside.

71
00:05:12,654 --> 00:05:14,996
[SPEAKER_03]: You are the perversion of perception, so to speak.

72
00:05:16,089 --> 00:05:17,869
[SPEAKER_03]: ...of the ownership.

73
00:05:18,189 --> 00:05:33,612
[SPEAKER_03]: But what you can do is start recognizing that the phenomenon of outside of this experience, as real as it is, whichever way it feels, whichever way it's perceived, is within the enduring experience as a whole.

74
00:05:34,012 --> 00:05:43,834
[SPEAKER_03]: So, instead of trying to manipulate how you refer to outside, you actually want to develop a greater context that includes even the most

75
00:05:45,115 --> 00:05:55,386
[SPEAKER_03]: External sense of experience that you might have in regard to sight, sound, smell, taste, touches, but it's... that's as such, is real, and as such a phenomenon, it's my experience as a whole.

76
00:05:55,446 --> 00:05:56,467
[SPEAKER_03]: It's the five aggregates.

77
00:05:57,848 --> 00:06:03,474
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you want to... want to broaden your context, as opposed to fix the notion of outside.

78
00:06:04,270 --> 00:06:12,955
[SPEAKER_02]: When Pandavera talks about this analogy of a parasite, like, the parasite is real, like, as such, in a sense.

79
00:06:12,975 --> 00:06:15,576
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not denying that there is a parasite.

80
00:06:15,636 --> 00:06:15,797
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

81
00:06:15,957 --> 00:06:19,759
[SPEAKER_02]: It's really seeing it as a parasite, and that means, yeah...

82
00:06:19,819 --> 00:06:20,179
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

83
00:06:20,279 --> 00:06:21,900
[SPEAKER_03]: Putting it in the context.

84
00:06:21,920 --> 00:06:24,521
[SPEAKER_03]: Putting it against the right backdrop, so to speak.

85
00:06:25,082 --> 00:06:26,422
[SPEAKER_03]: And right backdrop would be that.

86
00:06:26,483 --> 00:06:26,843
[SPEAKER_03]: Hold on.

87
00:06:27,561 --> 00:06:41,891
[SPEAKER_03]: I have the notion outside, that the world exists after me, the world will continue existing, the world existed before, but all those notions are on the basis of conceiving outside of this experience as a whole, yet they are experience as a whole.

88
00:06:41,911 --> 00:06:51,858
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, when you say, oh, the world exists, will exist after my death, that is your thought, here and now, what you are confined with, what you're experiencing it within these five aggregates.

89
00:06:52,338 --> 00:06:53,499
[SPEAKER_03]: You're still thinking your thoughts,

90
00:06:54,358 --> 00:07:00,037
[SPEAKER_03]: On the basis of your sixth sense base experience, and as such, that is within the Enduring Five Aggregates.

91
00:07:00,579 --> 00:07:04,921
[SPEAKER_02]: But, like, ignorance is beginningness, so you already are, like, it's not like...

92
00:07:05,361 --> 00:07:06,182
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, yeah, yeah.

93
00:07:06,262 --> 00:07:08,683
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not like you didn't do it and then you started one morning.

94
00:07:08,703 --> 00:07:09,303
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

95
00:07:09,744 --> 00:07:12,625
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the whole, like, Mūla Pāryāyā, like, conceiving.

96
00:07:12,645 --> 00:07:14,686
[SPEAKER_03]: It is, exactly, exactly, yeah.

97
00:07:14,726 --> 00:07:20,389
[SPEAKER_03]: He conceives in, apart from, he conceives as mine, fundamentally.

98
00:07:20,709 --> 00:07:27,693
[SPEAKER_03]: So, all these subtle conceivings of outside, first is second, second is first,

99
00:07:29,945 --> 00:07:37,948
[SPEAKER_03]: In all areas of your five-sense experience and the mind, all that kind of adds up, because it's beginningless.

100
00:07:39,469 --> 00:07:42,249
[SPEAKER_03]: And the practical result of that is me and the world.

101
00:07:43,730 --> 00:07:45,530
[SPEAKER_03]: Things that are mine and I am.

102
00:07:45,771 --> 00:07:47,991
[SPEAKER_03]: Sense of I am, sense of ownership, sense of self.

103
00:07:48,571 --> 00:07:49,892
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the result of this conceiving.

104
00:07:51,353 --> 00:07:57,858
[SPEAKER_03]: But again, if you read the Mūla Paryāya Sutta, again, somebody might assume, okay, so I must individually undo these little things.

105
00:07:58,518 --> 00:07:59,219
[SPEAKER_03]: That's impossible.

106
00:07:59,739 --> 00:08:05,143
[SPEAKER_03]: They're just subtle aspects that precede your attitude of mind.

107
00:08:06,104 --> 00:08:11,869
[SPEAKER_03]: So, undo the attitude of mind in regard to the five sense bases and the thoughts.

108
00:08:13,710 --> 00:08:17,693
[SPEAKER_03]: Undo the attitude just to the extent of mind, so you're not undoing the thoughts.

109
00:08:17,933 --> 00:08:20,195
[SPEAKER_03]: Just... there is a thought of the external world,

110
00:08:22,155 --> 00:08:27,783
[SPEAKER_03]: Undo, here and now, ownership of the experience as a whole in regard to the thought of the external world.

111
00:08:28,624 --> 00:08:37,596
[SPEAKER_03]: That will maintain the balance of thoughts, sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, and in them is just thoughts, sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches.

112
00:08:38,497 --> 00:08:38,858
[SPEAKER_03]: That's it.

113
00:08:39,953 --> 00:08:41,154
[SPEAKER_03]: Not too much, not too little.

114
00:08:41,814 --> 00:08:44,575
[SPEAKER_03]: But it all converges on that sense of mine.

115
00:08:45,036 --> 00:08:51,039
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you want to fulfill the Mūla Pāryāya Sutta, you basically practice not mine in regard to everything top to bottom.

116
00:08:51,639 --> 00:08:53,900
[SPEAKER_03]: Past, future, present, doesn't matter in what direction.

117
00:08:54,440 --> 00:09:02,844
[SPEAKER_03]: Whatever has arisen, whatever is real as such, as an image, as a feeling, as a perception, not mine to the extent that it... for as long as it endures, it won't be mine.

118
00:09:03,265 --> 00:09:03,885
[SPEAKER_03]: And then the next thing.

119
00:09:04,345 --> 00:09:05,025
[SPEAKER_03]: And then the next thing.

120
00:09:05,166 --> 00:09:05,866
[SPEAKER_03]: And then the next thing.

121
00:09:07,559 --> 00:09:11,241
[SPEAKER_03]: And you would have... you would have diminished all of the conceivings from all of our eyes with them.

122
00:09:12,102 --> 00:09:18,566
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what would be a necessary prerequisite for practicing not-mind in regard to everything?

123
00:09:18,606 --> 00:09:22,368
[SPEAKER_03]: Sight, sounds, smell, taste, touches, thoughts, while they endure.

124
00:09:23,289 --> 00:09:24,810
[SPEAKER_03]: What would be the necessary prerequisite?

125
00:09:24,830 --> 00:09:28,612
[SPEAKER_03]: What do you have to do in order to... for this practice to even become intelligible?

126
00:09:30,634 --> 00:09:31,214
[SPEAKER_01]: Establishment.

127
00:09:31,982 --> 00:09:43,269
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, like, and I'll keep talking about it for as long as I live, because it's easy to hear this, okay, so I'll start doing this, yet your whole physical behavior, verbal behavior, remains completely unpurified.

128
00:09:44,478 --> 00:09:46,900
[SPEAKER_03]: Still polluted, not clear from blemishes.

129
00:09:47,501 --> 00:09:51,865
[SPEAKER_03]: Those are the contradictions of mind, of outside, of lust, aversion, delusion.

130
00:09:52,405 --> 00:09:53,826
[SPEAKER_02]: You are conceiving a subject.

131
00:09:54,167 --> 00:09:54,527
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

132
00:09:54,567 --> 00:10:01,633
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if you are perpetuating conceivings through your actions and contemplating on the level of mind, unmindful and not mind, that's... that doesn't work.

133
00:10:02,434 --> 00:10:05,557
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, basically, you have to recognize that your views are

134
00:10:06,943 --> 00:10:07,423
[SPEAKER_01]: The result.

135
00:10:07,543 --> 00:10:09,464
[SPEAKER_01]: Perpetuated through your actions.

136
00:10:09,484 --> 00:10:10,184
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

137
00:10:10,364 --> 00:10:11,585
[SPEAKER_01]: The result of those actions.

138
00:10:11,845 --> 00:10:19,587
[SPEAKER_01]: And they're much more coarse and could not even be recognized as views until you stop acting in those ways.

139
00:10:19,707 --> 00:10:20,088
[SPEAKER_01]: Absolutely.

140
00:10:20,128 --> 00:10:30,071
[SPEAKER_01]: And only when you stop acting in those ways can you recognize, oh, actually, just by doing that, without even thinking about it, that's the view that I'm affirming, basically.

141
00:10:30,331 --> 00:10:30,591
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

142
00:10:31,091 --> 00:10:35,633
[SPEAKER_03]: Through development of virtue and sense restraint, you are then looking at

143
00:10:37,648 --> 00:10:38,489
[SPEAKER_03]: Add a bigger picture.

144
00:10:39,749 --> 00:10:48,614
[SPEAKER_03]: And now, within that, you start making effort to cultivate the attitude of not-mind in, as I said, in whatever's felt, whatever's perceived.

145
00:10:48,995 --> 00:10:54,958
[SPEAKER_03]: So, again, it's not about shaping, manipulating or changing what is felt or what is perceived.

146
00:10:55,538 --> 00:11:03,663
[SPEAKER_03]: It's about extending the attitude of not-mind in regard to the whatever precious felt, whatever perception is being perceived.

147
00:11:04,878 --> 00:11:07,521
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I shall guard the attitude of not mine.

148
00:11:08,061 --> 00:11:08,442
[SPEAKER_03]: That's it.

149
00:11:08,662 --> 00:11:09,383
[SPEAKER_03]: That's my work.

150
00:11:09,463 --> 00:11:10,404
[SPEAKER_03]: That's my threshold.

151
00:11:10,464 --> 00:11:10,784
[SPEAKER_03]: That's...

152
00:11:10,804 --> 00:11:11,044
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't...

153
00:11:11,104 --> 00:11:12,206
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't go past that point.

154
00:11:13,006 --> 00:11:13,867
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the gateway.

155
00:11:15,149 --> 00:11:15,569
[SPEAKER_03]: And you can.

156
00:11:15,629 --> 00:11:24,879
[SPEAKER_03]: That's exactly why the Buddha then did encourage the noble disciples, Sotāpanna's or higher, who have, sort of, accomplished themselves in virtue.

157
00:11:26,215 --> 00:11:34,200
[SPEAKER_03]: Sense restraint, he said, okay, you should thus go around in your day-to-day existence and every... whatever you experience, you should regard it.

158
00:11:34,580 --> 00:11:36,901
[SPEAKER_03]: This is not mine, this is not I am.

159
00:11:38,803 --> 00:11:49,149
[SPEAKER_03]: And the reason why it's in that order, this is not mine, this is not I am, it's because your sense of I am, your sense of self, is the result.

160
00:11:49,884 --> 00:11:52,425
[SPEAKER_03]: So, not for me, not mine, not I am.

161
00:11:52,505 --> 00:11:54,226
[SPEAKER_03]: Do I need to repeat it as a mantra?

162
00:11:54,266 --> 00:11:54,326
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

163
00:12:11,147 --> 00:12:12,968
[SPEAKER_03]: I need to repeat it if I forgot about it.

164
00:12:13,648 --> 00:12:15,969
[SPEAKER_03]: But even then, I don't do it for the sake of repeating it.

165
00:12:16,049 --> 00:12:19,850
[SPEAKER_03]: I do it for the sake of re-establishing that context of, oh, not mine.

166
00:12:21,430 --> 00:12:21,911
[SPEAKER_03]: Not for me.

167
00:12:22,051 --> 00:12:23,451
[SPEAKER_03]: In regard... not for me, exactly.

168
00:12:23,531 --> 00:12:27,292
[SPEAKER_03]: So, there is this feeling present and that is trying to pressure me to act.

169
00:12:28,433 --> 00:12:30,594
[SPEAKER_03]: Not for me, the pressure to act.

170
00:12:31,234 --> 00:12:33,815
[SPEAKER_03]: Not like, oh, not mine, feeling impermanent, anicca, anicca.

171
00:12:33,835 --> 00:12:34,195
[SPEAKER_03]: No, no, no.

172
00:12:34,515 --> 00:12:35,295
[SPEAKER_03]: Don't try to get rid of it.

173
00:12:35,335 --> 00:12:35,895
[SPEAKER_03]: It's permanent.

174
00:12:35,975 --> 00:12:36,375
[SPEAKER_03]: It's real.

175
00:12:36,415 --> 00:12:37,496
[SPEAKER_03]: It's going to pressure me forever.

176
00:12:38,447 --> 00:12:39,208
[SPEAKER_03]: That's not a problem.

177
00:12:39,668 --> 00:12:45,872
[SPEAKER_03]: The problem is, if I take it as mine, at any point of that eternity, that's when it becomes a problem.

178
00:12:46,132 --> 00:12:52,136
[SPEAKER_03]: If I don't take it as mine, well, eternity is seen as impermanent, is seen as unownable.

179
00:12:52,316 --> 00:12:53,097
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what's the problem?

180
00:12:54,097 --> 00:12:56,059
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you are free from the eternal saṃsāra.

181
00:12:57,059 --> 00:12:57,439
[SPEAKER_03]: There you go.

182
00:12:59,681 --> 00:13:04,304
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you know, the world is presenting... it's presented as for you.

183
00:13:05,285 --> 00:13:07,166
[SPEAKER_03]: It's pointing... all pointing at you.

184
00:13:07,914 --> 00:13:16,706
[SPEAKER_03]: Pressuring you, trying to move you, feelings, things that, as I said, you perceive, memories, future plans, doesn't matter.

185
00:13:16,767 --> 00:13:24,678
[SPEAKER_03]: It's all for me, for me, for me, and you just take for granted that, oh, there must be this me, there must be this guy, that all these things point at.

186
00:13:25,714 --> 00:13:27,816
[SPEAKER_03]: But if you realize, well, that's another thought, that's for me.

187
00:13:28,096 --> 00:13:28,516
[SPEAKER_03]: Conceiving.

188
00:13:28,536 --> 00:13:36,001
[SPEAKER_03]: The image of myself, the image of a guy there, the owner of this, is another thing that's for me, which means, oh, so I could actually disown him.

189
00:13:36,342 --> 00:13:48,470
[SPEAKER_00]: People are always asking, you know, is there that external world, or is it just the mind, or, you know, where am I in position of this, this world that lasts for forever, that goes through these cycles?

190
00:13:49,591 --> 00:13:56,839
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, when I die, and then I go to this realm, and that realm, I travel within that, and so on, and this, and that, and the other...

191
00:13:56,859 --> 00:14:02,185
[SPEAKER_03]: The answer to all of those questions is there is suffering, and there is freedom from it.

192
00:14:03,587 --> 00:14:09,854
[SPEAKER_03]: If you have such questions after you would have fully resolved liability to suffering, so after you're an arahant,

193
00:14:10,655 --> 00:14:11,996
[SPEAKER_03]: Then you can come and we can talk about it.

194
00:14:12,916 --> 00:14:14,637
[SPEAKER_03]: Everything else is a complete waste of time.

195
00:14:14,657 --> 00:14:17,358
[SPEAKER_00]: There is that sutra where that monk disrobes, doesn't he?

196
00:14:17,378 --> 00:14:19,639
[SPEAKER_00]: He disrobes, he says the Buddha is wrong or something like that.

197
00:14:20,600 --> 00:14:29,404
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... Oh, I will disrobe or... unless the Buddha tells me whether the world is external or eternal or not.

198
00:14:29,424 --> 00:14:30,264
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

199
00:14:31,385 --> 00:14:35,286
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the Buddha says, well, did I ever say I was going to tell you those things?

200
00:14:35,326 --> 00:14:36,687
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, come and I'll tell you that, yeah.

201
00:14:38,228 --> 00:14:38,608
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, it's...

202
00:14:39,518 --> 00:14:40,498
[SPEAKER_03]: Is that similar to the arrow?

203
00:14:40,639 --> 00:15:07,430
[SPEAKER_03]: You get shot, a person gets shot by somebody, they bring a surgeon that's really there, right there, and to treat him and save his life by pulling the arrow, dealing with what must be dealt there and then, without wasting any time, but he won't let him do that, he won't follow his instruction to get the arrow out, until he finds out who shot him.

204
00:15:08,205 --> 00:15:08,966
[SPEAKER_03]: Why he shot him?

205
00:15:09,106 --> 00:15:10,707
[SPEAKER_03]: How tall was the person that shot him?

206
00:15:11,327 --> 00:15:13,068
[SPEAKER_03]: What was his political affiliation?

207
00:15:13,569 --> 00:15:15,810
[SPEAKER_03]: What was his religion?

208
00:15:16,131 --> 00:15:17,031
[SPEAKER_03]: Why did he shoot him?

209
00:15:17,351 --> 00:15:18,232
[SPEAKER_03]: What was his childhood?

210
00:15:18,332 --> 00:15:19,393
[SPEAKER_03]: What were the reasons for it?

211
00:15:20,373 --> 00:15:21,274
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he dies.

212
00:15:21,534 --> 00:15:21,995
[SPEAKER_00]: Bleeds out.

213
00:15:22,115 --> 00:15:27,098
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the only reason he's asking those questions is because he has been shot.

214
00:15:27,198 --> 00:15:27,378
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

215
00:15:27,558 --> 00:15:28,459
[SPEAKER_00]: He's suffering.

216
00:15:29,080 --> 00:15:31,421
[SPEAKER_00]: I need to figure it out because I'm suffering.

217
00:15:31,461 --> 00:15:35,284
[SPEAKER_03]: Those questions only arise because those questions are the habits

218
00:15:36,531 --> 00:15:44,779
[SPEAKER_03]: And, and, and, and mental sort of patterns that you developed on account of experiencing suffering, not knowing how to deal with it.

219
00:15:46,188 --> 00:15:52,610
[SPEAKER_02]: So, coming out of suffering is a broader intention to any intention, for example, asking that question?

220
00:15:53,530 --> 00:16:10,616
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, yeah, the broadest intention is... You realize this is whatever... Like, see, if he were to have time to reflect there why he was dying and bleeding out because he shot with an arrow, he would realize, wait, but the only reason I'm asking all these questions is because this arrow hurts me.

221
00:16:11,312 --> 00:16:20,934
[SPEAKER_03]: And the only ways of me, in the past, dealing with any pain, is finding satisfaction through senses or thinking and justifications, assumptions and views.

222
00:16:21,434 --> 00:16:24,475
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the sole reason for those things, that I was doing them.

223
00:16:25,235 --> 00:16:27,275
[SPEAKER_03]: It was just to cover up and manage suffering.

224
00:16:28,175 --> 00:16:36,997
[SPEAKER_03]: So, there is a surgeon right in front of me, who says, no, he can actually save my life, and it seems that he can,

225
00:16:38,703 --> 00:16:46,027
[SPEAKER_03]: And the only thing he's asking me is to refrain wasting time on those questions and actually start practicing.

226
00:16:47,248 --> 00:16:47,908
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you...

227
00:16:47,968 --> 00:16:50,670
[SPEAKER_03]: I can have those questions, but no.

228
00:16:51,210 --> 00:16:52,271
[SPEAKER_03]: I'll put them aside.

229
00:16:52,431 --> 00:16:54,492
[SPEAKER_03]: I'll put my mind thinking those questions.

230
00:16:54,512 --> 00:16:57,714
[SPEAKER_03]: I'll put that aside while I'm doing what needs to be done.

231
00:16:57,734 --> 00:16:58,955
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, you don't have to get rid of them yet.

232
00:16:58,975 --> 00:16:59,195
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

233
00:16:59,475 --> 00:17:04,438
[SPEAKER_03]: So, when people start asking such questions, pretty much the only thing, like, they would...

234
00:17:05,160 --> 00:17:12,366
[SPEAKER_03]: The main thing that would determine whether there is even a... whether there can even be a conversation taking place is, do you keep the precepts?

235
00:17:12,887 --> 00:17:13,567
[SPEAKER_03]: Are you celibate?

236
00:17:13,668 --> 00:17:14,528
[SPEAKER_03]: Are you sense-restrained?

237
00:17:14,568 --> 00:17:15,809
[SPEAKER_03]: Are you guarding your sense-doors?

238
00:17:16,030 --> 00:17:17,591
[SPEAKER_03]: Are you moderate in eating?

239
00:17:18,492 --> 00:17:23,877
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, you would be able to understand the place where those views are, the roots of those questions.

240
00:17:24,017 --> 00:17:27,179
[SPEAKER_03]: Even if you're mistakenly asking them, you could actually then understand

241
00:17:28,113 --> 00:17:30,175
[SPEAKER_03]: Why that is wrong, from the point of view of practice.

242
00:17:30,855 --> 00:17:45,568
[SPEAKER_03]: But if a person is not making any effort, or minimal effort, or occasional effort, not purified in virtue, not guarding the sense doors, but is revolving and emphasizing these questions all the time, any answer to that, it's not a practice, it's not a dhamma, it won't make a difference.

243
00:17:46,281 --> 00:17:54,929
[SPEAKER_03]: That's often, like, people would kind of have the same attitude towards, oh, what's the Buddhist perspective on doing such and such thing in such and such circumstance?

244
00:17:54,989 --> 00:17:55,910
[SPEAKER_03]: There is no Buddhist.

245
00:17:56,150 --> 00:17:59,132
[SPEAKER_03]: Buddhist perspective is suffering and freedom from it.

246
00:17:59,593 --> 00:18:07,920
[SPEAKER_03]: Which means, Buddhist perspective is uprooting of the four upādānas, uprooting of sensuality, abandoning of desire, overcoming of attachment.

247
00:18:08,040 --> 00:18:09,282
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the Buddhist perspective.

248
00:18:09,922 --> 00:18:19,188
[SPEAKER_03]: But, you know, what do I do, like, if this happens and I see that... Well, if you're not free from desire, not free from attachment, not free from ownership, whatever you do will be wrong.

249
00:18:22,390 --> 00:18:25,893
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the Buddha wouldn't be helping them in any way by indulging?

250
00:18:26,413 --> 00:18:26,553
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

251
00:18:26,733 --> 00:18:31,276
[SPEAKER_03]: He might help them in worldly, like, somebody's not interested in practicing the dhamma and tell me what to do.

252
00:18:31,336 --> 00:18:34,578
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, keep the precepts, be a good person, don't harm others.

253
00:18:34,718 --> 00:18:35,099
[SPEAKER_03]: Do that.

254
00:18:36,069 --> 00:18:38,070
[SPEAKER_03]: But see, they don't ask the questions like that.

255
00:18:38,170 --> 00:18:39,890
[SPEAKER_03]: People usually ask the questions... No, no, no.

256
00:18:39,930 --> 00:18:41,531
[SPEAKER_03]: From the dhamma point of view.

257
00:18:41,551 --> 00:18:45,572
[SPEAKER_03]: There is no dhamma point of view that tells you how to lead your household life.

258
00:18:46,232 --> 00:18:48,373
[SPEAKER_03]: Dhamma point of view tells you you have to abandon it.

259
00:18:48,933 --> 00:18:52,774
[SPEAKER_03]: You have to abandon everything, approve all of the attachments, be free from craving.

260
00:18:52,815 --> 00:18:54,075
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the dhamma point of view.

261
00:18:55,620 --> 00:18:56,401
[SPEAKER_02]: There's no compromise.

262
00:18:56,421 --> 00:18:56,601
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

263
00:18:56,701 --> 00:19:06,892
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if somebody just asks you, like, an advice how to live a skillful lay life, sure, but don't confuse that for the dhamma practice or coming from the dhamma practice, because it doesn't.

264
00:19:06,912 --> 00:19:09,094
[SPEAKER_03]: So, also, again, with these questions...

265
00:19:09,154 --> 00:19:17,343
[SPEAKER_03]: The principle of sensory strain comes first, applies even to these thoughts, even to these, like, just just mental processes of

266
00:19:18,322 --> 00:19:19,363
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, who shot me?

267
00:19:19,423 --> 00:19:20,143
[SPEAKER_03]: Why he shot me?

268
00:19:20,163 --> 00:19:20,543
[SPEAKER_03]: And all of this.

269
00:19:20,563 --> 00:19:20,803
[SPEAKER_03]: All of this.

270
00:19:20,823 --> 00:19:21,224
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's like, wait.

271
00:19:21,264 --> 00:19:29,167
[SPEAKER_03]: But see, in order to overcome the pain, because of which I'm asking these questions, I need to stop emphasizing those questions first.

272
00:19:29,328 --> 00:19:31,088
[SPEAKER_00]: It's, it's, it's, it's a scratching.

273
00:19:31,449 --> 00:19:32,069
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just scratching.

274
00:19:32,129 --> 00:19:32,329
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

275
00:19:32,789 --> 00:19:33,290
[SPEAKER_00]: It's exactly the same.

276
00:19:33,310 --> 00:19:33,830
[SPEAKER_00]: Scratching the mind.

277
00:19:33,850 --> 00:19:34,770
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the next question.

278
00:19:34,830 --> 00:19:36,171
[SPEAKER_03]: It's a, it's a, it's a scratch.

279
00:19:36,211 --> 00:19:36,591
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

280
00:19:36,711 --> 00:19:40,293
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you have, you have your, your physical scratches and so on, verbal ones.

281
00:19:40,353 --> 00:19:41,454
[SPEAKER_03]: But yeah, this is a mental one.

282
00:19:41,954 --> 00:19:42,554
[SPEAKER_03]: And then it's, oh

283
00:19:45,023 --> 00:19:45,723
[SPEAKER_03]: Endure the pressure.

284
00:19:45,903 --> 00:19:48,084
[SPEAKER_03]: See, because it's going to be like pressure of doubt.

285
00:19:48,745 --> 00:19:49,205
[SPEAKER_03]: But why?

286
00:19:49,465 --> 00:19:49,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Who?

287
00:19:49,765 --> 00:19:50,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

288
00:19:50,145 --> 00:19:50,625
[SPEAKER_01]: What for?

289
00:19:51,186 --> 00:19:51,806
[SPEAKER_03]: What did I do?

290
00:19:51,906 --> 00:19:52,666
[SPEAKER_03]: How did it happen?

291
00:19:54,347 --> 00:20:03,291
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's like, yeah, well, that pressure, those thoughts, has to remain and I have to endure them and not actually give in to, yeah, let me just answer and resolve this.

292
00:20:03,971 --> 00:20:06,312
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's how I will get closer to uprooting the pain.

293
00:20:07,832 --> 00:20:13,735
[SPEAKER_03]: Because if you have been withdrawn from it sufficiently, then, yes, you will be able to apply what the Buddha said.

294
00:20:13,795 --> 00:20:14,015
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, yeah.

295
00:20:15,118 --> 00:20:18,019
[SPEAKER_03]: I take it as not mine, not for me, not mine, not I am.

296
00:20:19,239 --> 00:20:25,760
[SPEAKER_03]: Even stuff that is, it could have not been, and it won't be.

297
00:20:25,880 --> 00:20:27,701
[SPEAKER_03]: Either way, it can't be for me.

298
00:20:28,181 --> 00:20:29,581
[SPEAKER_03]: Either way, it cannot be mine.

299
00:20:35,963 --> 00:20:37,903
[SPEAKER_01]: And basically, sensory strength is the

300
00:20:42,405 --> 00:20:47,388
[SPEAKER_01]: The necessary basis for even applying, for being able to apply that attitude.

301
00:20:47,448 --> 00:20:48,008
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

302
00:20:48,068 --> 00:21:01,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Because those things that you have to give up just in order to establish basic sense of strength are the things that you'd be most pressured into taking as your own.

303
00:21:01,675 --> 00:21:05,057
[SPEAKER_03]: So... Well, there is a reason exactly that those things always come with the pressure.

304
00:21:06,198 --> 00:21:10,560
[SPEAKER_03]: The ownership needs maintenance, craving.

305
00:21:12,952 --> 00:21:19,636
[SPEAKER_03]: And the only reason, again, that you would go... act out of pressure, is because it's painful.

306
00:21:21,197 --> 00:21:29,582
[SPEAKER_03]: If you stop to think about it, it's like, wait, but why... what is so bad in not following sensual desire?

307
00:21:30,943 --> 00:21:32,324
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, why don't I do it just for fun?

308
00:21:33,405 --> 00:21:33,605
[SPEAKER_01]: What?

309
00:21:33,765 --> 00:21:34,185
[SPEAKER_01]: Not follow?

310
00:21:34,265 --> 00:21:34,986
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, not follow.

311
00:21:35,066 --> 00:21:36,747
[SPEAKER_03]: Why don't I just, for fun, be celibate,

312
00:21:37,576 --> 00:21:42,038
[SPEAKER_03]: Not eat in the afternoon, live withdrawn from sensual pressures, just for fun.

313
00:21:42,118 --> 00:21:42,758
[SPEAKER_03]: Why don't I do it?

314
00:21:45,019 --> 00:21:47,941
[SPEAKER_00]: You consider everything is not for me.

315
00:21:48,041 --> 00:21:50,162
[SPEAKER_03]: Even leave that for later.

316
00:21:50,342 --> 00:21:54,944
[SPEAKER_03]: Just for fun, let me just not do those... those... act out of those pressuring things.

317
00:21:56,305 --> 00:21:57,745
[SPEAKER_03]: Why nobody does that just for fun?

318
00:21:57,865 --> 00:21:59,146
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, because it's very painful.

319
00:22:00,627 --> 00:22:03,828
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, that's surely... that tells you it's a problem.

320
00:22:05,068 --> 00:22:13,723
[SPEAKER_03]: If something is basically forcing you through pain to maintain it, that's... that's not a good deal.

321
00:22:16,723 --> 00:22:19,205
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's painful just to think about it.

322
00:22:19,445 --> 00:22:19,605
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

323
00:22:19,625 --> 00:22:21,106
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just... Of course, exactly.

324
00:22:21,126 --> 00:22:24,788
[SPEAKER_03]: So, even thinking about it, theoretically, abstractly, it's painful.

325
00:22:25,048 --> 00:22:28,130
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, you do dismiss even the possibility of that you could do it.

326
00:22:28,310 --> 00:22:28,530
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

327
00:22:29,111 --> 00:22:34,314
[SPEAKER_03]: But say, say you do think about it, and say, you do realize, yeah, this is painful, isn't it?

328
00:22:34,474 --> 00:22:36,355
[SPEAKER_03]: And, why am I maintaining?

329
00:22:37,856 --> 00:22:42,679
[SPEAKER_03]: If you stop doing it, it's like, what... what do you have to lose?

330
00:22:43,839 --> 00:22:44,520
[SPEAKER_03]: What would change?

331
00:22:50,520 --> 00:23:06,973
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if you're acting out of pressuring sensualities, enduring the pressure, feels like all this pain will last forever if I don't scratch the itch, but then you still don't scratch the itch... You're not a slave, but... Well, then, yeah, then the pressure starts to diminish.

332
00:23:08,034 --> 00:23:08,394
[SPEAKER_03]: It has to.

333
00:23:08,434 --> 00:23:13,478
[SPEAKER_03]: And you kind of know, even before you start, that it would, just maybe not as quickly as you would want it to.

334
00:23:15,400 --> 00:23:15,640
[SPEAKER_03]: But...

335
00:23:16,618 --> 00:23:22,660
[SPEAKER_03]: It does start to diminish, because it requires your input, it requires fueling, it requires maintenance.

336
00:23:24,840 --> 00:23:25,760
[SPEAKER_03]: And you stop feeding it.

337
00:23:28,721 --> 00:23:37,224
[SPEAKER_03]: And again, that's why Buddha said it is possible, even for an outsider, outside of his teaching, his dispensation, to become fully free from this sensuality.

338
00:23:38,484 --> 00:23:45,746
[SPEAKER_03]: Through not feeding it, overcome the pressure, and see the actual ridiculous trapping

339
00:23:47,310 --> 00:23:52,356
[SPEAKER_03]: Devious nature of it and be completely dispassionate towards it.

340
00:23:52,696 --> 00:23:53,437
[SPEAKER_03]: See, it is a trap.

341
00:23:55,439 --> 00:23:56,340
[SPEAKER_03]: A ridiculous trap.

342
00:24:04,770 --> 00:24:05,871
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, dispassion is...

343
00:24:13,055 --> 00:24:28,968
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, but I think, practically, you could say, this passion is directly proportional to your ability to endure the pain of the pressure.

344
00:24:29,328 --> 00:24:31,610
[SPEAKER_01]: And that would then reveal the views, yeah.

